Chapter: SyntaxHighlight
  cites Foreword chapter CCE

 Codex source, classified into coloured runs. This is the half of
 syntax highlighting that has nothing to do with a screen: it takes one
 line of source and answers where each token starts, how long it is, and
 what kind of thing it is. Drawing is somebody else's job, which is what
 lets this be tested without a framebuffer.

 THE INDENT DECIDES EVERYTHING, and it decides it before any character
 is looked at. `scan-token` in the compiler's lexer branches on
 `s.column == 2`: a line whose text begins one space in is PROSE and its
 contents are never tokenized, and one more space makes the same line
 code. So this classifier counts leading spaces first and only then asks
 what the characters are. Getting that order wrong produces a highlighter
 that colours the word `cites` inside an English sentence, which is the
 same confusion the compiler itself raises CDX3010 for.

   zero spaces   a Chapter: or Section: header
   one space     prose, whole line, no tokens inside it
   two or more   code

 EVERY CHARACTER TEST GOES THROUGH THE CCE PREDICATES. Codex text is CCE
 internally, not ASCII: `a` is 15 and `e` is 13, digits are 3 to 12, and
 a space is 2. A classifier written against ASCII ranges answers that no
 line contains a letter, which is the bug `text-to-upper` shipped with
 for as long as it did. So `is-letter`, `is-digit`, `is-upper` and
 `is-whitespace` come from Foreword chapter CCE, and the three
 punctuation characters this file needs are derived from Text literals
 with `syn-ch` rather than written as numbers. A literal's characters are
 already CCE, so the derivation cannot drift the way a hardcoded 45 for a
 hyphen would.

 We say:

Section: Classes

 SynPlain is the honest answer for anything unrecognised, and it exists
 so that an unclassified character is drawn in the foreground colour
 rather than dropped. A highlighter that silently omits what it does not
 understand loses source off the screen.

  SynClass =
   | SynPlain
   | SynProse
   | SynHeader
   | SynDirective
   | SynKeyword
   | SynType
   | SynName
   | SynNumber
   | SynString
   | SynOperator

  SynRun = record {
   sr-start : Integer,
   sr-len : Integer,
   sr-class : SynClass
  }

 One letter per class, for tests and for reading a classification at a
 glance. The runtime path never calls this.

  syn-class-tag : SynClass -> Text
  syn-class-tag (k) =
   when k
    is SynPlain -> "."
    is SynProse -> "p"
    is SynHeader -> "h"
    is SynDirective -> "d"
    is SynKeyword -> "k"
    is SynType -> "T"
    is SynName -> "n"
    is SynNumber -> "9"
    is SynString -> "s"
    is SynOperator -> "o"

Section: Characters

 The three characters this file must recognise by identity rather than
 by category, derived from literals so they are CCE by construction.

  syn-ch : Text -> Integer
  syn-ch (t) = char-code (char-at t 0)

  syn-hyphen : Integer
  syn-hyphen = syn-ch "-"

  syn-dquote : Integer
  syn-dquote = syn-ch "\""

  syn-hash : Integer
  syn-hash = syn-ch "#"

  syn-backslash : Integer
  syn-backslash = syn-ch "\\"

  syn-is-word-char : Text, Integer -> Boolean
  syn-is-word-char (s) (i) =
   let c = char-at s i
   in is-letter c | is-digit c

Section: Indent

  syn-indent : Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  syn-indent (s) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then i
   else if is-whitespace (char-at s i) then syn-indent s (i + 1) len
   else i

Section: Keywords

 The reserved word list, as `docs/DevelopersGuide.md` states it. `as` is
 deliberately absent: it is not reserved, it is the idiomatic name for a
 list, and colouring it as a keyword would be wrong in most of the tree.

 `cites`, `quotes`, `grounds` and `trusting` are split out as directives
 rather than keywords because they are the chapter header's vocabulary
 and a reader looks for them as a block. `above` stays a keyword: it is
 only ever the tail of `trusting above N`, and colouring a bare `above`
 as a directive would be a lie in any other position.

  syn-directives : List Text
  syn-directives = ["cites", "quotes", "grounds", "trusting"]

  syn-keywords : List Text
  syn-keywords = [
   "let", "in", "if", "then", "else", "when", "is", "otherwise", "act", "end",
   "record", "mutable", "punctual", "unit", "above",
   "claim", "proof", "qed", "forall", "exists", "induction",
   "linear", "effect", "where", "with", "between", "and", "such", "that",
   "class", "instance", "lazy", "True", "False"
  ]

  syn-in-list : List Text, Text, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  syn-in-list (xs) (w) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then False
   else if list-at xs i == w then True
   else syn-in-list xs w (i + 1) len

Section: Classifying One Word

 A word that begins with an upper-case letter is a type, which is the
 whole of Codex's naming convention: types are PascalCase and values are
 kebab-case, so the first character settles it with no table.

  syn-classify-word : Text -> SynClass
  syn-classify-word (w) =
   if syn-in-list syn-directives w 0 (list-length syn-directives) then SynDirective
   else if syn-in-list syn-keywords w 0 (list-length syn-keywords) then SynKeyword
   else if text-length w == 0 then SynPlain
   else if is-upper (char-at w 0) then SynType
   else SynName

Section: Token Ends

 A hyphen continues a name only when a letter or a digit follows it,
 which is exactly the compiler's rule and is what keeps `x - 1` a
 subtraction, `a-2` one identifier, and `a->b` an arrow rather than a
 name called `a-`.

  syn-word-end : Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  syn-word-end (s) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then i
   else if syn-is-word-char s i then syn-word-end s (i + 1) len
   else if char-code (char-at s i) == syn-hyphen
         & i + 1 < len
         & syn-is-word-char s (i + 1)
    then syn-word-end s (i + 2) len
   else i

  syn-number-end : Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  syn-number-end (s) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then i
   else if is-digit (char-at s i) | is-letter (char-at s i) then syn-number-end s (i + 1) len
   else i

 A string runs to its closing quote, and a backslash inside it consumes
 the character after it so an escaped quote does not end the run. An
 unterminated string ends at the end of the line rather than running off
 it: a half-typed line is the normal state of an editor buffer, and a
 classifier that faulted on one would be useless in the place it is most
 wanted.

  syn-string-end : Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  syn-string-end (s) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then i
   else if char-code (char-at s i) == syn-backslash then syn-string-end s (i + 2) len
   else if char-code (char-at s i) == syn-dquote then i + 1
   else syn-string-end s (i + 1) len

Section: Classifying A Line

  syn-line : Text -> List SynRun
  syn-line (s) =
   let len = text-length s
   in let ind = syn-indent s 0 len
   in if ind >= len then []
   else if ind == 0 then [SynRun { sr-start = 0, sr-len = len, sr-class = SynHeader }]
   else if ind == 1 then [SynRun { sr-start = 1, sr-len = len - 1, sr-class = SynProse }]
   else syn-code-runs s ind len []

  syn-code-runs : Text, Integer, Integer, List SynRun -> List SynRun
  syn-code-runs (s) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else if is-whitespace (char-at s i) then syn-code-runs s (i + 1) len acc
   else syn-code-token s i len acc

  syn-code-token : Text, Integer, Integer, List SynRun -> List SynRun
  syn-code-token (s) (i) (len) (acc) =
   let c = char-at s i
   in let code = char-code c
   in if code == syn-dquote then
     let e = syn-string-end s (i + 1) len
     in syn-code-runs s e len (list-push acc (SynRun { sr-start = i, sr-len = e - i, sr-class = SynString }))
   else if code == syn-hash then
     let e = syn-number-end s (i + 1) len
     in syn-code-runs s e len (list-push acc (SynRun { sr-start = i, sr-len = e - i, sr-class = SynNumber }))
   else if is-digit c then
     let e = syn-number-end s i len
     in syn-code-runs s e len (list-push acc (SynRun { sr-start = i, sr-len = e - i, sr-class = SynNumber }))
   else if is-letter c then
     let e = syn-word-end s i len
     in let w = substring s i (e - i)
     in syn-code-runs s e len (list-push acc (SynRun { sr-start = i, sr-len = e - i, sr-class = syn-classify-word w }))
   else syn-code-runs s (i + 1) len (list-push acc (SynRun { sr-start = i, sr-len = 1, sr-class = SynOperator }))

Section: Rendering A Classification

 One tag character per source character, gaps included, so a test can
 compare a whole line's classification as a string. This is what makes
 the classifier falsifiable without a screen: the expected output names
 every character's class, so a token that grows, shrinks or changes kind
 moves the line.

  syn-tags : Text -> Text
  syn-tags (s) = syn-tag-fill (syn-line s) (text-length s) 0 0 ""

  syn-tag-fill : List SynRun, Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  syn-tag-fill (runs) (len) (i) (ri) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else if ri >= list-length runs then syn-tag-fill runs len (i + 1) ri (acc & " ")
   else let r = list-at runs ri
   in if i < r.sr-start then syn-tag-fill runs len (i + 1) ri (acc & " ")
   else if i < (r.sr-start) + (r.sr-len) then syn-tag-fill runs len (i + 1) ri (acc & syn-class-tag (r.sr-class))
   else syn-tag-fill runs len i (ri + 1) acc

Section: Colours

 XRGB, and deliberately a fixed palette rather than a Theme lookup. The
 GOP text primitive takes a packed colour and nothing on that path
 carries a Theme; wiring one through to reach six constants would be an
 abstraction for a single implementation, which is the thing
 `VisionAndVirtues` virtue 7 says not to build. When a second consumer
 wants different colours, that is the moment for a parameter.

  syn-colour : SynClass -> Integer
  syn-colour (k) =
   when k
    is SynPlain -> #D8D8D8
    is SynProse -> #7FA87F
    is SynHeader -> #E8C86A
    is SynDirective -> #C88ED8
    is SynKeyword -> #6AA9E8
    is SynType -> #6AD8C0
    is SynName -> #D8D8D8
    is SynNumber -> #E8916A
    is SynString -> #C0D86A
    is SynOperator -> #9AA0A8
